1. Introduction to JUnit 5 Testing Framework : 
 - JUnit 5 is the latest version of the popular Java testing framework used for unit testing Java applications.

 - It helps developers:

   - Test Java code automatically
   - Find bugs early
   - Improve code quality
   - Support Test-Driven Development (TDD)

 - JUnit is widely used with:

    - Spring Boot
    - Maven
    - Gradle
    - IntelliJ IDEA
    - Eclipse
    - VS Code

2. Unit Testing :
    - Unit Testing is a software testing technique where individual units or components of an application are tested independently.
    - A unit can be: 
        - A method
        - A function
        - A class
   Traditional Testing :
   - Traditional Testing usually refers to manual testing or broader testing approaches where the complete application is tested as a whole.
   - It includes:
        - Manual Testing
        - Integration Testing
        - System Testing
        - End-to-End Testing
   - Example: A tester manually checks whether: Login page works, Dashboard loads and Database stores data properly

3. Manual Unit Testing: 
   - Demonstrates manual unit testing steps without a testing framework
   - Involving: 
        - method invocation, 
        - input preparation, 
        - result verification, and 
        - developer alerting

4. Benefits of JUnit 5:
   - 1. Easy and Simple to Use
        - JUnit 5 provides:
           - Simple annotations
           - Clean syntax
           - Readable test cases
        - Example:
            @Test
            void testAddition() {
                assertEquals(10, 5 + 5);
            } 
   - 2. Improved Annotation Support
        - JUnit 5 introduces powerful annotations like:
           - Annotation	    Purpose
             @Test	        Defines test method
             @BeforeEach	Runs before each test
             @AfterEach	    Runs after each test
             @DisplayName	Custom test name
             @Disabled	    Skips tests
           - Example:
               @DisplayName("Login Test")
               @Test
               void loginTest() {
                   assertTrue(true);
               }
   - 3. Better Assertions
        - JUnit 5 provides enhanced assertion methods for validation.
        - Common assertions:
           - assertEquals()
           - assertTrue()
           - assertFalse()
           - assertThrows()
        - Example: 
           assertThrows(
               ArithmeticException.class,
               () -> {
                   int x = 10 / 0;
               }
           );
   - 4. Supports Lambda Expressions 
        - JUnit 5 fully supports Java 8 features like Lambda expressions. 
        - Example: 
           assertThrows(
               ArithmeticException.class,
               () -> System.out.println(10 / 0)
           );
        - This makes code shorter and cleaner.
   - 5. Modular Architecture
      - JUnit 5 is divided into modules:
         - Module	        Purpose
         - JUnit Platform	Provides APIs, runs testing and reports
         - JUnit Jupiter	Main testing API(writes Junit tests)
         - JUnit Vintage	Supports old JUnit tests
   - 6. Backward Compatibility
      - JUnit 5 can run:
         - JUnit 3 tests
         - JUnit 4 tests
      - using the Vintage engine.
      - This helps organizations migrate gradually.
   - 7. Dynamic Tests
      - JUnit 5 supports creating tests dynamically at runtime.
      - Example:
           @TestFactory
           Collection<DynamicTest> dynamicTests() { 
               return Arrays.asList(
                   DynamicTest.dynamicTest(
                       "Addition Test",
                       () -> assertEquals(4, 2 + 2)
                   )
               );
           }
   - 8. Nested Testing
      - Nested tests improve organization.
      - Example:
           @Nested
           class LoginTests {
           
               @Test
               void validLogin() {
               }
           
               @Test
               void invalidLogin() {
               }
           }
   - 9. Parameterized Testing
      - Run the same test with multiple inputs.
      - Example:
           @ParameterizedTest
           @ValueSource(ints = {1, 2, 3})
           void testNumbers(int number) { 
               assertTrue(number > 0);
           } 
      - Benefits:
           - Reduces duplicate code
           - Improves test coverage
   - 10. Faster Test Execution
      - JUnit 5 supports:
           - Parallel execution
           - Better performance optimization
      - This reduces testing time in large applications.
   - 11. Better IDE Support
      - JUnit 5 works smoothly with: 
           - IntelliJ IDEA
           - Eclipse
           - VS Code
           - NetBeans
      - Features include: 
           - One-click test execution
           - Debugging
           - Test reports
   - 12. CI/CD Integration
      - JUnit 5 integrates with: 
           - Jenkins
           - GitHub Actions
           - GitLab
      - This enables automated testing pipelines.